@subsection A Language for Describing Register Machines

Data-path and controller diagrams are adequate for representing simple machines
such as @abbr{GCD}, but they are unwieldy for describing large machines such
as an evaluator.  To make it possible to deal with complex machines, we
will create a language that presents, in textual form, all the information
given by the data-path and controller diagrams.  In this edition the language
is typed OCaml data: a controller is a list of @code{instruction} values, and
the simulator assembles it.

We define the data paths of a machine by declaring the registers and the
operations.  A register is named; an operation is named and computes a word
from its inputs, which are registers or constants.  Labels identify entry
points in the controller.  An instruction is one of the following, each a
constructor of @code{Sec_5_1.instruction}:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{Assign (register, source)} pushes a data-path button to assign a value
to a register.  The source is @code{Const word}, @code{Reg register}, or
@code{Label_ref label}.  (This corresponds to a box in the controller
diagram.)

@item
@code{Assign_op (register, operation, inputs)} assigns the value of an
operation on its inputs.

@item
A @code{Test (operation, inputs)}, that performs a specified test.

@item
A conditional branch (@code{Branch label}) to a location indicated by a
controller label, based on the result of the previous test.  (The test and
branch together correspond to a diamond in the controller diagram.)  If the
test is false, the controller continues with the next instruction in the
sequence.  Otherwise, the controller continues with the instruction after
the label.

@item
An unconditional branch (@code{Goto label}) naming a controller label at
which to continue execution, or @code{Goto_reg register} continuing at the
label the register holds.

@item
@code{Save register} and @code{Restore register} push and pop the stack.

@item
@code{Perform (operation, inputs)} runs an action for its effect.

@end itemize

@noindent
The machine starts at the beginning of the controller instruction sequence and
stops when execution reaches the end of the sequence.  Except when a branch
changes the flow of control, instructions are executed in the order in which
they are listed.

@noindent
@ref{Figure 5.3} shows the @abbr{GCD} machine described in this way.  This
example only hints at the generality of these descriptions, since the
@abbr{GCD} machine is a very simple case.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Figure 5.3}Figure 5.3:} @math{\downarrow} A specification of the @abbr{GCD}
machine.

@example ocaml
let gcd_controller =
  [ Label "test-b"
  ; Test ("=", [ Reg "b"; Const (Int 0) ])
  ; Branch "gcd-done"
  ; Assign_op ("t", "rem", [ Reg "a"; Reg "b" ])
  ; Assign ("a", Reg "b")
  ; Assign ("b", Reg "t")
  ; Goto "test-b"
  ; Label "gcd-done"
  ]
;;

let gcd controller a b =
  run
    ~registers:[ "a"; "b"; "t" ]
    ~operations:arith_operations
    ~inputs:[ "a", Int a; "b", Int b ]
    ~controller
    "a"
;;
(* gcd gcd_controller 206 40 answers 2 *)
@end example

@end quotation

@noindent
The operations come from the section's shared arithmetic table.  To make the
controller easier to read we name it, but it is an ordinary list: labels mark
positions and execute nothing, and @code{assemble} turns it into an
instruction array with a label table, rejecting a label defined twice.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.2}Exercise 5.2:} Use the register-machine language
to describe the iterative factorial machine of @ref{Exercise 5.1}, as a typed
instruction list over the arithmetic operations.
@end quotation

@subsubheading Actions

Let us modify the @abbr{GCD} machine so that we can supply the numbers
whose @abbr{GCD} we want and get the answer printed.  A printing operation
writes its rendered operand and a newline through the machine's emit
sink.@footnote{This assumption glosses over a great deal of complexity.
Usually a large portion of the implementation of a real system is dedicated
to making reading and printing work.}
It does not produce an output value to be stored in a register.  Though it
has an effect, this effect is not on a part of the machine
we are designing.  We will refer to this kind of operation as an
@newterm{action}.  To run an action from the controller we use
@code{Perform}.  Thus, the action of printing the contents of register
@code{t} is the instruction

@example ocaml
Perform ("print", [ Reg "t" ])
@end example

@noindent
@ref{Figure 5.4} shows the data paths and controller for the new @abbr{GCD}
machine.  Instead of having the machine stop after printing the answer, we
have made it start over, so that it repeatedly takes a pair of numbers,
computes their @abbr{GCD}, and prints the result.  This structure is like the
driver loops we used in the interpreters of @ref{Chapter 4}.

@float
@anchor{Figure 5.4}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 5.4:} A @abbr{GCD} machine that reads inputs and prints results.

@example
                   .--------.
                    \ read /
                     \____/
                       |
               +-------*------+
               |              |
        a<-rd (X)            (X) b<-rd
               |              |
               V              V           ___
            +-----+        +-----+       /   \
            |  a  |<--(X)--+  b  +----->|  =  |
            +-+-+-+  a<-b  +-+---+       \___/
              | |            |  ^          ^
           +--+ +----+    +--+  |          |
           |         |    |    (X) b<-t   / \
           V         V    V     |        / O \
      .---------.  .---------.  |       /_____\
--(X)->\ print /    \  rem  /   |
   P    \_____/      \_____/    |
                        |       |
                       (X) t<-r |
                        |       |
                        V       |
                     +-----+    |
                     |  t  +----+
                     +-----+
@end example

@example ocaml
let gcd_print_controller =
  [ Assign_op ("t", "rem", [ Reg "a"; Reg "b" ])
  ; Perform ("print", [ Reg "t" ])
  ; Label "loop"
  ; Test ("=", [ Reg "b"; Const (Int 0) ])
  ; Branch "done"
  ; Assign_op ("t", "rem", [ Reg "a"; Reg "b" ])
  ; Assign ("a", Reg "b")
  ; Assign ("b", Reg "t")
  ; Goto "loop"
  ; Label "done"
  ]
;;
(* on a = 206 and b = 40 the machine prints 6, then answers a = 2 *)
@end example

@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap5/Fig5.4c,,147mm,Figure 5.4: A GCD machine that reads inputs and prints results.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap5/Fig5.4c.std,,147mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 5.4:} A @abbr{GCD} machine that reads inputs and prints results.}
